course: dictionary
lesson: 9665
title: Subtitles
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A subtitle is a written line of words, usually at the bottom of a film or TV screen. It shows what people say, so you can read it as you watch. «Dubbing means recording new voices in another language; some countries prefer subtitles, the written words at the bottom of the screen.» The word «written» is a clue: subtitles are words you read, not sounds you hear.
explain: Subtitles can also mean a second, smaller title in a book or article. It sits under the main heading and gives more detail. If a book is called "Volcanoes" and its subtitle is "How the Earth Makes Fire", the subtitle tells you what the book is really about.
explain: Subtitle can be a verb too. To subtitle a film is to add the subtitles to it. Look at the endings: a subtitle, subtitles, subtitled, subtitling. The word changes its ending to show whether you mean one thing, many things, the past, or the action happening now.
fact: A subtitle is written words shown on a film or TV screen.
fact: Subtitles help deaf people and people learning a language.
fact: Subtitles let you watch films made in other languages.
fact: In a book or article, a subtitle is a smaller title that gives more detail.
fact: «Closed captions» is the American name for subtitles.
exercise: «Some countries prefer subtitles, the written words at the bottom of the screen.» Where are subtitles shown? | Inside the speaker's ears | In a music box | On the front cover of a book | *At the bottom of the screen
exercise: What does a subtitle in a book or article add? | A picture of the author | A full stop at the end | A recording of new voices | *A second, smaller title that gives more detail
exercise: «They subtitled the film so that everyone could follow it.» Find and copy one word that is a form of subtitle. | *subtitled | everyone | follow | film
test: Which phrase asks someone to show the words at the bottom of a film? | Turn up the volume | *Turn on the subtitles | Press the red button | Switch off the light
test: Why might a deaf person use subtitles? | To hear the music better | *So they can read what people say | To learn to draw | To make the film longer
exam: «Dubbing means recording new voices in another language; subtitles are the written words at the bottom of the screen.» What is the main difference? | *Dubbing changes the voices, while subtitles write the words on screen | Dubbing is a smaller title for a book | Subtitles change the voices, while dubbing writes the words on screen | They mean exactly the same thing
